## Why This Is Asked

This asks for a practical, beginner-friendly AWS AI/ML workflow that combines text detection, translation, sentiment, storage, and a lightweight read API, while emphasizing security and cost.

## Key Concepts

- DetectDominantLanguage (Comprehend)
- TranslateText (Translate)
- DetectSentiment (Comprehend)
- S3 → Lambda → Step Functions orchestration
- DynamoDB indexing by documentId
- API Gateway + Lambda for reads
- IAM least privilege and KMS
- Privacy: PII masking, encryption, and retention

## Code Example

```javascript
import { ComprehendClient, DetectDominantLanguageCommand, DetectSentimentCommand } from "@aws-sdk/client-comprehend";
import { TranslateClient, TranslateTextCommand } from "@aws-sdk/client-translate";

const comprehend = new ComprehendClient({region:"us-east-1"});
const translate = new TranslateClient({region:"us-east-1"});

async function analyzeText(text){
  const dl = await comprehend.send(new DetectDominantLanguageCommand({Text: text}));
  const langCode = (dl.Languages?.[0]?.LanguageCode) ?? "en";
  let english = text;
  if (langCode !== "en"){
    const tr = await translate.send(new TranslateTextCommand({Text: text, SourceLanguageCode: langCode, TargetLanguageCode: "en"}));
    english = tr.TranslatedText;
  }
  const sentiment = await comprehend.send(new DetectSentimentCommand({Text: english, LanguageCode: "en"}));
  return { language: langCode, translated: english, sentiment: sentiment.Sentiment };
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this with mocks and local development?
- What are the implications for cost at scale and how would you mitigate?
- How would you handle a batch of emails arriving concurrently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a simple, real-world data processing pipeline using AWS AI services with proper data handling and cost awareness. It checks event-driven orchestration, service selection, and basic data modeling.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven Lambda workflows triggered by S3 uploads
- Textract (PDF text extraction) vs Rekognition (image analysis)
- Metadata storage and DynamoDB upsert for result persistence
- IAM least privilege, DLQ for error handling, encryption, and access logs
- Cost-conscious design with input validation, async processing, and data retention policies

## Code Example
```javascript
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const record = event.Records[0];
  const bucket = record.s3.bucket.name;
  const key = decodeURIComponent(record.s3.object.key.replace(/\+/g, ' '));
  const fileType = key.split('.').pop().toLowerCase();

  if (fileType === 'pdf') {
    const textractJob = await textract.startDocumentTextDetection({ DocumentLocation: { S3Object: { Bucket: bucket, Name: key } } }).promise();
    // Store jobId and poll for results
  } else if (['jpg', 'png', 'jpeg'].includes(fileType)) {
    const rekognitionResults = await rekognition.detectLabels({ Image: { S3Object: { Bucket: bucket, Name: key } }).promise();
    // Process labels
  }
};
```

Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a scalable, privacy-aware, vendor-integrated NLP pipeline that processes structured chat data in JSONL format, with PII redaction, cross-account sharing, and cost-aware inference choices.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven architectures (S3 events, Step Functions)
- Lambda for lightweight processing and redaction
- Amazon Comprehend for NER/Sentiment
- SageMaker for topic modeling and batch inference
- Data governance: IAM, KMS, encryption, access control
- Observability: CloudWatch, X-Ray, metrics, traces
- Cross-account sharing patterns and vendor approvals

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lambda: simple PII redaction sketch
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const body = JSON.parse(event.body);
  body.messages = body.messages.map(m => m.replace(/[A-Z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Z0-9.-]+\.[A-Z]{2,4}/gi, '[REDACTED_EMAIL]'));
  return { statusCode: 200, body: JSON.stringify(body) };
};
```

